organizers of the tokyo olympics have  announced a total ban  on spectators in the stadiums it follows  the declaration of a new state of  emergency in tokyo because of increasing  numbers of coronavirus cases  foreign fans had already been barred now  japanese spectators are being told to  stay at home  the games are due to begin in just over  two weeks and will be the first ever  olympics held behind closed doors  the olympic torch continued its long  journey towards tokyo to open the games  later this month while there were plenty  of fans cheering on the torchbearers as  they pass through the saitama prefecture  rising covet cases in the capital means  there won't be any fans to greet them  in tokyo government officials have  announced a state of emergency in tokyo  set to begin next week in a bid to curb  the spread of the virus  while the crux of the measure is cutting  the sale of alcohol at bars and  restaurants to help limit social  activities it will also directly affect  the olympics  although the number of severely ill  patients and the occupancy rates of  hospital beds have remained at a low  level  the impact of the variant strains must  be taken into account  we must strengthen the counter measures  to prevent the infections  from spreading to the rest of the  country again  while olympic organizers had already  capped spectators to 50 percent of venue  capacity and barred international fans  from attending  the new ruling now prevents any  spectators from attending the games  though public sentiment in japan has  widely turned against hosting the  tournament  olympic organizers have dug in their  heels and are committed to seeing the  games through  we continue to  be committed to our principle of  organizing  safe olympic and paralympic games  together  with you and we  have shown you know this responsibility  since the day of the postponement  the games are estimated to have cost  more than 20 billion dollars cancelling  them which should cut off broadcasting  revenue  would make recouping any of that next to  impossible for tokyo  and the international olympic committee  for better or for worse  just like the olympic torch the games  look set to march on  let's get more from mark meadows from dw  sport welcome  mark um comet is responsible for all  sorts of unprecedented uh  things over this last couple of years um  now we have a spectator-free  olympics what sort of games then are we  in for  a very very strange one uh as you say  we've got used to this idea of not  having fans in stadiums over the last 18  months especially with football  but when it comes to the biggest  sporting event of all that only comes  around every four years  it's a big big blow it's a big blow for  the japanese athletes  because they were really counting on  having japanese fans in the stands  pushing them along japanese fans tend to  be very passionate very excited  we already knew that spectators from  abroad weren't going to be there but  still  it's going to change the whole sort of  ethos of the games really i mean you  think about when you  when you watch it even on television the  fans are part of the games  maybe when you're watching archery or  rowing you don't see the fans quite so  much but in the really big sports like  athletics  and swimming especially the noise when  it comes to swimming  the fans are a big big part of it so  it's going to feel very very strange  so well you you you mentioned the  football there so we're we're in the  middle of the european football  championships we've seen large crowds  we've seen large crowds at wembley  while london is in the middle of an  increasing number of coronavirus  infections so what is the difference  between the two events the two cities  i guess the japanese are being extra  cautious  uh it is true that in britain the  vaccine rate is higher than in  in japan so japan fears that if the  delta variant really uh  variant really grips there then they  could be in in serious trouble  and they decided they have no other  option even though as you say the cases  are  relatively low in compared to what we're  seeing in  in europe um i think also there's  political dynamics in there there's an  election coming up in  in japan in october uh but other people  will say people in europe are being too  rash i mean 60 000  fans at these england games at wembley  when cases are rocketing maybe in a  month two months time people will  realize that actually  japan took a very sensible course of  action right and the the international  olympic committee  is determined to press ahead with this  they have to  uh it's all about money basically i mean  it's cost billions and billions to host  the games  the postponement cost extra now they're  not getting ticket income  the only income they're getting is from  sponsors and from television  and if we're honest the sponsors care  about the tv viewers  not the spectators in the stands that's  how they make their money  and so that is basically what the ioc is  relying on  if they scrap these games the rrc would  be in  big big bother especially with the next  olympics the winter olympics being in  beijing  and various governments talking about  diplomatic boycotts  the best case scenario is an olympics  without fans  where at least you have the sport and  you have the tv viewers okay  thank you for that mark meadows dw sport
 

